  "prediction": "**Summary of the Holman Rule Report**\n\nThe Holman rule is a congressional rule that separates policy and funding decisions in the House of Representatives. Established in 1835, the rule prohibits the inclusion of legislative provisions in appropriation bills unless they \"retrench expenditures\" by reducing the number and salary of federal officials, the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury, or the amounts of money covered in an appropriation bill.\n\nThe rule has undergone several changes over the years, with the most significant revision occurring in 1983. However, in 2017, the House reinstated the rule with additional language that expanded the scope of retrenchments to include reductions in the number and salary of federal officials and the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury.\n\nThe report highlights the evolution of the Holman rule and its application in various congressional sessions. It notes that the rule is not intended to circumscribe Congress's lawmaking authority but rather provides a limited exception to the general prohibition against legislation in appropriation measures.\n\nThe report also discusses the precedents established by the House Parliamentarian, which have incrementally narrowed the application of the Holman rule. These precedents include the requirement that retrenchments must be specific and not contingent on an event, and that they must apply only to funds appropriated in the pending general appropriations bill.\n\nThe report concludes that the Holman rule is intended to provide Members with additional tools to reduce spending during consideration of the regular general appropriation bill. However, it notes that the rule is subject to the general requirement for germaneness and that broad legislative provisions are unlikely to be allowed under the rule.\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n* The Holman rule separates policy and funding decisions in the House of Representatives.\n* The rule prohibits the inclusion of legislative provisions in appropriation bills unless they \"retrench expenditures.\"\n* The rule has undergone several changes over the years, with the most significant revision occurring in 1983.\n* The 2017 reinstatement of the rule expanded the scope of retrenchments to include reductions in the number and salary of federal officials and the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury.\n* The rule is subject to the general requirement for germaneness and is not intended to open the door for legislative provisions that would expand the scope of the bill.",
